This is the exercism forth exercise. Key points:

- `Forth(input []string) ([]int, error)` — each element of input is a line of text.
- Words are case-insensitive.
- Definition: `: name tokens ;` — the definition body must be captured at the point of definition (for the "can use different words with the same name" test: `: foo 5 ;`, `: bar foo ;`, `: foo 6 ;`, `bar foo` → `5 6`). So the body must be stored as tokens (a list of words), and resolution happens at execution time. Also "can define word that uses word with the same name": `: foo 10 ;`, `: foo foo 1 + ;`, `foo` → 11. Since the body is a list of tokens, `foo` inside the body resolves to the new definition (10) at execution time. If we eagerly expanded the body at definition time, the second definition would fail (foo undefined) or resolve incorrectly. So store tokens and resolve at execution time.

- Numbers: a sequence of one or more digits, possibly with a leading `-`. Per the spec, "a number is a sequence of one or more (ASCII) digits" — but the tests include negative numbers. Standard exercism solution: a number is an optional `-` followed by digits.

- Errors: empty stack, only one value on the stack, division by zero, undefined operation, illegal operation (redefining a number).

- The expected value for `"1 drop"` is `[]int{}` — an empty non-nil slice. Note: `reflect.DeepEqual(v, tc.expected)` — DeepEqual([]int{}, []int(nil)) is false, so we must return an empty slice, not nil, on success.

- Definition parsing: `: name ... ;` — tokens within a single line. If `;` is missing, error. If a word is defined, the name must not be a number.

